Ladybird preserves each original shaping run when shortening mixed-
direction text for a standard block ellipsis. It retains run boundaries
with the soft wrap offsets, and shapes each retained range with its
original text type. LineBoxFragmentData::append_glyph_run combines the
glyphs, preserving their directional placement. Normal fragment merging
and legacy glyph-based truncation are unchanged.

Blink reserves ellipsis space in LineBreaker::UpdateAvailableWidth.
LineBreaker::ShapeText reshapes each InlineItem with its own direction.
WebKit reserves space in LineBuilder::layoutInlineContent, keeping the
directional runs through its trailingBlockEllipsis helper. Gecko’s
TextOverflow clips nsTextFrame content through MeasureCharClippedText,
using its directional gfxTextRun. All three preserve directional shaping
rather than assigning one direction to merged mixed-direction text.

The regression combines an opening parenthesis with Hebrew grapheme
clusters under word-break:break-all and standard line-clamp. Chrome’s
experimental implementation matches the explicit retained-text reference
at three widths. The unchanged Ladybird branch fails that comparison
when ellipsis placement requires reshaping an internal prefix.
